er, positive scale, cover and diameter conditions, and nonempty-set supremum cost; no normalization factor is present. It uses real exponent and implicit EMetricSpace, measurable space and BorelSpace. However, the candidate's identification of content limits and conversions from this formula are not themselves licensed just by citing the formula; see findings.\",\"locations\":[\"nl0.json:188-215\",\"nl0.json:695-702\",\"nl0.json:733-740\",\"signature-evidence.json:152-179\",\"Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Measure/Hausdorff.lean:535-552\"]},\"M009\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"Full implicit measurable-space/measure telescope and pointwise function inequality match the official monotonicity theorem. Application is valid in form if its pointwise slicing-cover inequality and measurable-space setup are established; the unsupported slicing argument is separately found.\",\"locations\":[\"nl0.json:217-242\",\"nl0.json:704-709\",\"signature-evidence.json:182-209\",\"Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Integral/Lebesgue/Basic.lean:84\"]},\"M010\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"Signature matches: measurable set premise, arbitrary ENNReal constant, indicator integral equals constant times measure. Application specifies the indicator and set; the claimed subsequent measure bound is not a consequence of M010 alone.\",\"locations\":[\"nl0.json:244-269\",\"nl0.json:712-717\",\"signature-evidence.json:212-239\"]},\"M011\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"Official theorem and full signature give volume of closed interval [a,b] as ofReal(b−a), with implicit real endpoints and no additional hypotheses. The application only provides this formula; the needed restricted-measure comparison and interval-length bounds require proof beyond this theorem.\",\"locations\":[\"nl0.json:271-294\",\"nl0.json:720-723\",\"signature-evidence.json:242-269\",\"Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Measure/Lebesgue/Basic.lean:86\"]},\"M012\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"Official theorem matches the full implicit EMetricSpace, measurable space, BorelSpace, set and NNReal exponent telescope, with non-top Hausdorff measure implying dimH≤↑d. The candidate's S001 application is appropriate after the asserted zero-measure result, but does not establish that result itself.\",\"locations\":[\"nl0.json:296-324\",\"nl0.json:726-731\",\"signature-evidence.json:272-299\",\"Mathlib/Topology/MetricSpace/HausdorffDimension.lean:123-124\"]}},\"structure_review\":{\"verdict\":\"FAIL\",\"notes\":\"The graph has one target and one producer R001; its listed inputs include the H roots and every retained M, and there are no D nodes. But the single producer does not constitute a valid proof: its exported statement omits the original parameter/hypothesis context in its displayed text, while the central proof contains unsupported steps. AND closure therefore fails mathematically. No per-inference packet replay is claimed.\",\"locations\":[\"spec.json:4-27\",\"nl0.json:5-325\",\"nl0.json:327-345\",\"nl0.json:572-648\"]},\"findings\":[{\"severity\":\"FAIL\",\"location\":\"nl0.json:598-599\",\"notes\":\"The slicing lower-bound argument does not establish the key pointwise inequality as written. A_i=π₁(V_i) only covers the slice E at height t when t∈B_i=π₂(V_i), not whenever t∈J_i. J_i is an enlargement of B_i, so the right-hand indicator sum can be zero for t∈J_i\\\\B_i while a covering member is needed to cover E at height t. The asserted cover by A_i with t∈J_i is therefore false as justified. This breaks the Tonelli estimate and the D>0 lower bound, and consequently the target proof's closure.\"},{\"severity\":\"FAIL\",\"location\":\"nl0.json:599\",\"notes\":\"The concluding infimum step is unsupported and in general goes in the wrong direction: a bound C_s(E,δ)≤Σ_iρ_i^(s+1)+ε for each cover V implies a bound by the infimum over all such covers only after taking infimum on the right-hand side; the text instead concludes C_s(E,δ)≤C_(s+1)(F,δ) without handling the ε, the cost convention/nonempty supremum, or the distinction between the exponent-s cover cost and exponent-(s+1) cost. More importantly, its preceding pointwise estimate already fails as noted.\"},{\"severity\":\"FAIL\",\"location\":\"nl0.json:575\",\"notes\":\"The upper bound is not a complete derivation from the declared inputs: the conversion from H^q(E)=0 to arbitrarily small-scale Nat-indexed covers of controlled q-cost, the zero-diameter replacement argument, and the product-cover construction/cost accounting are asserted rather than proved. The declared calculation policy only permits elementary distance/diameter estimates, not these Hausdorff-cover existence and measure-content implications. This prevents accepting the upper-bound branch as a closed NL0 argument.\"},{\"severity\":\"FAIL\",\"location\":\"nl0.json:598-599; nl0.json:650-723\",\"notes\":\"The lower-bound analytic application also skips necessary measure details: it asserts restricted Lebesgue mass estimates from an enclosing closed interval without stating/proving measure monotonicity, and uses restricted mass of [0,1] as one without an application establishing that value. These do not follow from M010 and M011 alone. M002's AEMeasurability obligation is only asserted for the indicator, without fully specifying the restricted measure-space structure.\"},{\"severity\":\"FAIL\",\"location\":\"nl0.json:575-599\",\"notes\":\"The critical-exponent limiting steps (q decreasing to D and s increasing to D), and the identification of divergent/vanishing scale contents with the corresponding Hausdorff measure via the displayed supremum formula, are described but not derived in a complete argument. The cited M008 formula supplies the definition/equality of the measure as a supremum/infimum, not these limit, approximation, and coercion arguments.\"}]}}","status":"completed"}],"error":null,"incomplete_details":null,"tools":[{"type":"function","name":"search_mathlib","description":"在固定 mathlib 的 Lean 源码中搜索正则表达式，返回至多 100 行。","parameters":{"type":"object","pr